def add(y,n,r,t,tags,s,sol,reason):new.append(dict(id=f'latam-ibero-{y}-{n}',country='International',competition='Ibero-American Mathematical Olympiad',year=y,number=str(n),statement=s,solution=sol,sourceUrl=urls[y],solutionUrl='',solutionKind='original-exposition',topic=t,tags=tags.split('|'),difficulty=r,ratingReason=reason,reviewStatus='individually-assessed',notes='International Ibero-American competition; organizer paper verified, statement restated and solution independently derived.'))
add(2025,2,4.5,'Combinatorics','Invariants|Weighted sums|Coordinates',r'An $n\times n$ board, $n\ge3$, initially has all $n^2$ coins in one cell. In a move, choose a cell with at least two coins and send two of its coins to opposite neighbouring cells: either horizontally, vertically, along a northeast–southwest diagonal, or along a northwest–southeast diagonal. All cells used must lie on the board. Suppose a sequence of moves leaves exactly one coin in every cell. Prove that the numbers of moves in the two diagonal directions are equal.',r'''Number rows and columns from $1$ to $n$. The sums of the row coordinates and column coordinates over all coins are invariant, because each move sends two coins to opposite neighbours. If the starting cell is $(r,c)$, the final sums therefore give $r=c=(n+1)/2$; in particular, the hypothesis can hold only for odd $n$.

Now consider $W=\sum xy$, summing the product of the coordinates over all coins. Initially $W=n^2((n+1)/2)^2$. Finally $W=(1+\cdots+n)^2$, the same value. Horizontal and vertical moves leave $W$ unchanged. Sending coins to $(x+1,y+1),(x-1,y-1)$ increases it by $2$, while sending them to $(x+1,y-1),(x-1,y+1)$ decreases it by $2$. Thus the two diagonal move counts must be equal.''','First moments locate the initial pile at the centre, after which a mixed coordinate moment distinguishes the diagonal directions. Choosing the second weighted sum is the key insight; simple coin counts or ordinary checkerboard parity do not record the required difference.')
add(2025,4,5.5,'Number theory','Sums of cubes|Primes|Divisibility|Casework',r'Find all prime pairs $p>q>1$ satisfying $\displaystyle\sum_{j=-q-1}^{q+1}(p+j)^3=(3pq)^2$.',r'''Pair terms around $p$ and use the sum of squares to obtain
$$(2q+3)\bigl(p^2+(q+1)(q+2)\bigr)=9pq^2.$$
Reducing modulo $p$ shows that $p$ divides $(2q+3)(q+1)(q+2)$.

If $q=2$, this forces $p=3$ or $7$; neither satisfies the displayed equation. Suppose $q$ is odd. Then $p$ is odd and $p>q$, so it cannot divide $q+1$, and if it divides $q+2$ it equals $q+2$. If it divides $2q+3$, the positive quotient is odd and smaller than $3$, so $p=2q+3$.

For $p=q+2$, cancellation gives $(2q+3)^2=9q^2$, hence $q=3$ and $p=5$. For $p=2q+3$, cancellation gives $4q^2-15q-11=0$, whose discriminant is $401$, not a square. Therefore the only pair is $\boxed{(5,3)}$, which satisfies the original equation.''','Symmetric expansion turns a long sum into a short divisibility condition. Because p exceeds q, primality sharply limits which factors can contain p. Careful treatment of q=2 and the two remaining algebraic cases gives completeness.')
add(2024,4,6,'Geometry','Constructions|Closure|Complex numbers|Centroids',r'Some points of the plane are red. Whenever two distinct red points $P,Q$ and a third point $X$ form a triangle with angles $30^\circ,60^\circ,90^\circ$ in any order, $X$ is red. Prove that if the three vertices of a nondegenerate triangle are red, its centroid is red.',r'''Identify the plane with the complex numbers. If $p,q$ are red and distinct, then each point $p+\lambda(q-p)$ is red for
$$\lambda=i\sqrt3,\quad \frac{i}{\sqrt3},\quad \alpha=\frac{1+i\sqrt3}{4},\quad\overline\alpha=\frac{1-i\sqrt3}{4},$$
because the corresponding triangle is $30^\circ$–$60^\circ$–$90^\circ$. For the last two coefficients, its side lengths are proportional to $1,1/2,\sqrt3/2$.

Composing the first two operations about the same point $p$ gives $p-(q-p)=2p-q$. Thus reflection in a red point preserves redness. Composing the $\alpha$ and $\overline\alpha$ operations gives $p+(q-p)/4$, because $\alpha\overline\alpha=1/4$; reflecting $p$ about this new point produces $(p+q)/2$. Hence midpoints of red points are red.

Applying the $i/\sqrt3$ operation twice gives $p-(q-p)/3$, whose reflection about $p$ is $p+(q-p)/3$. Thus a point one-third along a segment with red endpoints is red.

Now let $m=(b+c)/2$. It is red. So is $u=a+(m-a)/3$. Reflecting $a$ in $u$ gives $2u-a=a+2(m-a)/3=(a+b+c)/3$, the centroid.''','The rule must be bootstrapped into affine constructions, rather than applied to the original triangle once. Two successive allowed right triangles give reflection, midpoint and trisection operations. Combining them constructs the centroid; finding these useful closure operations is the main difficulty.')
